{"kind":"artifact","text":"The wire-type parity gate at internal/wireparity as main carries it, at merge commit 8924b4d09fde8ebb2705deb127334d56baa94f3e. It landed the work approved by codex in ae256854 and ratified in 1a881c1a, and it is what main now runs.\n\nThe package is a single Go test, so it runs in CI wherever the suite runs and needs no separate wiring. For each Go wire type it takes the field names encoding/json actually emits — resolving tags, omitempty and unexported fields the way the encoder does, then round-tripping through Marshal so a malformed tag cannot pass by being misparsed twice the same way — and compares them with the names the matching TypeScript interface in ui/src/lib/api.ts declares, reporting the difference in both directions.\n\nIt compares names, not types, deliberately. Names are the failure that is silent: a renamed or dropped json tag turns a working read into undefined at run time, and TypeScript cannot catch it, because the hand-written mirror is the only thing it checks against.\n\nThe gap it cannot close by itself is stated where the gap is: the Go-type-to-interface pairs live in a single map beside the types they guard, so adding a wire type without adding it there leaves that type unguarded. An explicit map was chosen over reflecting over the package because a silent miss is exactly what this gate exists to prevent.\n\nVerified at this merge commit in the main checkout: go test ./internal/... -count=1 passes, internal/wireparity included.\n\nThis retires 5656492a, the candidate artifact at this path on branch head be1564d7, which main now contains. No tracked file cites it. It rests on 64976636 at ui, the mirror it guards, which is live and not stale.","body":{"commit":"8924b4d09fde8ebb2705deb127334d56baa94f3e","path":"internal/wireparity"}}